# 3. HCI buffer types and memory layout

## `struct bt_buf_s`

File:

`/home/mi/vela_competition/contest/nuttx/include/nuttx/wireless/bluetooth/bt_buf.h:92-111`

```c
struct bt_buf_s
{
  FAR struct bt_buf_s *flink;

  union
  {
    struct bt_buf_hci_data_s hci;
    struct bt_buf_acl_data_s acl;
  } u;

  FAR uint8_t *data;
  uint8_t len;
  uint8_t pool;
  uint8_t ref;
  uint8_t type;

  FAR struct iob_s *frame;
};
```

Important details:

- `data` points to the first byte of the HCI packet.
- `len` is the HCI packet length beginning at `data`.
- `frame` owns the backing `iob_s`.
- `ref` is a reference count; use `bt_buf_addref()` and `bt_buf_release()`.
- `len` is only `uint8_t` in this tree.
- `frame->io_len` includes the IOB offset, while `bt_buf_s::len` excludes the reserved headroom.

## Allocation and reserved headroom

Prototype:

`/home/mi/vela_competition/contest/nuttx/include/nuttx/wireless/bluetooth/bt_buf.h:156-158`

```c
FAR struct bt_buf_s *bt_buf_alloc(enum bt_buf_type_e type,
                                  FAR struct iob_s *iob,
                                  size_t reserve_head);
```

When no IOB is supplied, allocation does:

`/home/mi/vela_competition/contest/nuttx/wireless/bluetooth/bt_buf.c:343-362`

```c
buf->frame->io_offset = reserve_head;
buf->frame->io_len    = reserve_head;
buf->frame->io_pktlen = reserve_head;

buf->data = buf->frame->io_data + reserve_head;
```

Therefore, for H:4:

```text
frame->io_data
        |
        +-- reserved H:4 byte(s)
        |
        +-- buf->data: HCI command/event/ACL packet
```

The helper functions are:

```c
bt_buf_extend(buf, len);   /* append bytes */
bt_buf_provide(buf, len);  /* prepend bytes from headroom */
bt_buf_consume(buf, len);  /* remove bytes from front */
bt_buf_tail(buf);          /* data + len */
```

Definitions:

`/home/mi/vela_competition/contest/nuttx/include/nuttx/wireless/bluetooth/bt_buf.h:206-260`

and implementation:

`/home/mi/vela_competition/contest/nuttx/wireless/bluetooth/bt_buf.c:528-615`.

`bt_buf_provide()` requires sufficient headroom:

`/home/mi/vela_competition/contest/nuttx/wireless/bluetooth/bt_buf.c:581-590`

```c
DEBUGASSERT(bt_buf_headroom(buf) >= len);
buf->data -= len;
buf->len  += len;
```

## HCI packet structures

Packed HCI headers are at:

`/home/mi/vela_competition/contest/nuttx/include/nuttx/wireless/bluetooth/bt_hci.h:287-309`

```c
struct bt_hci_evt_hdr_s
{
  uint8_t evt;
  uint8_t len;
};

struct bt_hci_acl_hdr_s
{
  uint16_t handle;
  uint16_t len;
};

struct bt_hci_iso_hdr_s
{
  uint16_t handle;
  uint16_t len;
};

struct bt_hci_cmd_hdr_s
{
  uint16_t opcode;
  uint8_t param_len;
};
```

For an outgoing command:

```text
data[0..1]       opcode, little endian
data[2]          parameter length
data[3..]        command parameters
```

For an outgoing ACL packet:

```text
data[0..1]       connection handle / packet boundary flags
data[2..3]       ACL payload length
data[4..]        ACL payload
```

There is no H:4 transport byte in `data`; the H:4 byte is in the reserved headroom if the lower driver uses H:4.

## Receiving packets

`bt_receive()` allocates a new stack buffer with `BLUETOOTH_H4_HDRLEN` headroom:

`/home/mi/vela_competition/contest/nuttx/wireless/bluetooth/bt_hcicore.c:1820-1830`

```c
buf = bt_buf_alloc(type, NULL, BLUETOOTH_H4_HDRLEN);
memcpy(bt_buf_extend(buf, len), data, len);
```

The constants are:

`/home/mi/vela_competition/contest/nuttx/include/nuttx/net/bluetooth.h:47-57`

```c
#define BLUETOOTH_H4_HDRLEN     1
#define BLUETOOTH_MAX_HDRLEN    9
#define BLUETOOTH_MAX_MTU       70
#define BLUETOOTH_MAX_FRAMELEN  (BLUETOOTH_MAX_MTU + BLUETOOTH_MAX_HDRLEN)
```

Current host processing accepts only `BT_EVT` and `BT_ACL_IN` in the main receive path:

`/home/mi/vela_competition/contest/nuttx/wireless/bluetooth/bt_hcicore.c:1832-1839`

```c
if (type != BT_ACL_IN)
  {
    if (type != BT_EVT)
      {
        ...
        return -EINVAL;
      }
  }
```

Although `BT_ISO_IN` and `BT_ISO_OUT` exist in the enum and several transports parse ISO packets, this host path rejects `BT_ISO_IN`. That is a significant version/tree-specific trap.

